#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105401 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9317eb-d1ce-5f42-b69b-a37e643b865b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Projection Using Angle and Magnitude"}] :question/id #uuid "3278333b-a29e-54c5-bc94-b5525bc6f751" :question/math-academy-id "e-14617" :question/problem "Suppose that $\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=8, \\mid \\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|=4$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$. Find the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ in terms of the vector $\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncomp_{b}a &= \\mid a \\mid \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 8\\cos (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-4\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{b}\\, \\mid} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{4} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= comp_{b}a \\cdot u \\\\\n&=-4\\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}b \\\\\n&=-\\sqrt{3}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0670172-77cb-5e88-b78d-e5136c06095f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Projection Using Components"}] :question/id #uuid "58b2269d-f998-5ba5-9df8-54ea422c43aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14618" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-3,5 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 5,-4,0 \\rangle$. Find the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{b \\cdot b}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{x}b_{x} + a_{y}b_{y} + a_{z}b_{z}}{b_{x}^{2} + b_{y}^{2} + b_{z}^{2}}b \\\\\n&= (1 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-4) + 5 \\cdot 0)/(5^{2} + (-4)^{2} + 0^{2})b \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{41}⟨5,-4, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{85}{41},-\\frac{68}{41}, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105403 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c96375-27d5-5ada-a952-34e09b3e92bf" :knowledge-point/title "Perpendicular Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "663f3f5e-56cf-5517-ba1e-d1a355767e0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14619" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$. Find the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{b \\cdot b}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{x}b_{x} + a_{y}b_{y} + a_{z}b_{z}}{b_{x}^{2} + b_{y}^{2} + b_{z}^{2}}b \\\\\n&= (1 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\cdot 5 + 1 \\cdot (-1))/(1^{2} + 5^{2} + (-1)^{2})b \\\\\n&= (0)/(1^{2} + 5^{2} + (-1)^{2})b \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
